gned}]}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -7 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}{{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{21}=7,{u}_{22}=-5,$ and ${l}_{21}\\cdot {u}_{12}=-35.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555769 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119793 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6093e24d-14bb-5e3b-a0b4-7a562a12f6a2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the LU Factorization of a Singular 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25d6bf9d-c36b-57f4-a4a3-d6d10d20a9fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555078} {:answer/id #uuid "2d113435-7d4d-5e64-bf7a-6096265af4ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555100} {:answer/id #uuid "97a99f95-7b5c-511f-b1dd-63628675fb7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555361} {:answer/id #uuid "ac5ab758-9dd9-5005-99ab-90aa7d7d9c95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555414} {:answer/id #uuid "b15e8623-fcd9-53a6-8695-28d9aee82949" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555427}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ac5ab758-9dd9-5005-99ab-90aa7d7d9c95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/