osition $A=LU,$ then $L=$" :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ 4 & -5\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{L}{{\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555856 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119793 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6093e24d-14bb-5e3b-a0b4-7a562a12f6a2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the LU Factorization of a Singular 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d7c47e5-bc18-5759-9dba-bef203ec6a76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555101} {:answer/id #uuid "382d6998-098c-5ac8-a4eb-2cf87144df8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555121} {:answer/id #uuid "49cdf79c-58f3-5591-94f0-0d48ea2d4f6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555158} {:answer/id #uuid "4da3e47c-22f5-5965-908e-6f3b266993d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555174} {:answer/id #uuid "c847da6e-0099-5446-9b32-bda4703a56ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555480}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "49cdf79c-58f3-5591-94f0-0d48ea2d4f6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555158} :answer-field/id #uuid "3bb19108-7f19-5591-ae3c-6544894088fe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555669}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d20ab7a7-2299-5eca-8c7e-9709ddaf034c" :question/math-academy-id "q-32547" :question/problem "Given that\n $A=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ -12 & -9\\end{aligned}]$ \nhas the LU decomposition $A=LU$, then ${l}_{21}+{u}_{22}=$" :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ -12 & -9\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}{{[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{21}=-3,{u}_{22}=0,$ and ${l}_{21}+{u}_{22}=-3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441355 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119853 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2c1a562-1b3d-5180-9fef-e20ea916b17a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying LU Factorizations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "289d8b11-5b33-5df3-83e4-bfbab449cfa4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441324} {:answer/id #uuid "374fcecf-456e-59e1-810b-f5c42633df35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441325} {:answer/id #uuid "55cc3f2d-2c9d-518f-83cf-c0d0c9b25d1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441329} {:answer/id #uuid "64d9eef5-56f0-5943-8389-0af8adc56d25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441332} {:answer/id #uuid "d54070f0-d20a-5ea6-aa9b-5964baf98a82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441345}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d54070f0-d20a-5ea6-aa9b-5964baf98a82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441345} :answer-field/id #uuid "407f9dd1-cf10-5a35-8a2f-095674048c4c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441348}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8cd9aa05-4a21-5975-bf09-f09975b43169" :question/math-academy-id "q-32611" :question/problem "Which of the following products is the LU factorization of a matrix $B?$\n\nI. $B=\\begin{bmatrix}12 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}11 & 17 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nII. $B=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 12 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}11 & 17 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nIII. $B=\\begin{bmatrix}11 & 17 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that in the LU factorization of a matrix $B,$ we write $B=LU$ where the matrix $L$ is a unit lower triangular matrix (a lower triangular matrix that has only $1$'s on the main diagonal) and the matrix $U$ is an upper triangular matrix:\n\n$$\nB=\\overset{L}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ ∗ & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot \\overset{U}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}∗ & ∗ \\\\ 0 & ∗\\end{bmatrix}}}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each product in turn.\n\n- The first product is *not* in $LU$ form because  the matrix on the left is not a unit lower triangular matrix, as its main diagonal contains numbers different from $1.$\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}12 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}11 & 17 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\times \n$$\n\n- The second product is in $LU$ form because the left matrix is a unit lower triangular matrix, and the right matrix is an upper triangular matrix.\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 12 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}11 & 17 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,✓\n$$\n\n- The third product is *not* in $LU$ form. Although we have a product of an upper triangular matrix on the left and a unit lower triangular matrix on the right, the order of multiplication is not correct (instead, we need a unit lower triangular matrix on the left and an upper triangular matrix on the right).\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}11 & 17 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\times \n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302266 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119792 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9279d979-74ba-5504-b98a-63692db3af72" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the LU Factorization of a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4374f2ca-d187-5c74-a8e5-694af7f1a10f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300294} {:answer/id #uuid "4a359ac0-f626-5fc9-b0c1-01e620a91911" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300341} {:answer/id #uuid "7a50d87f-d58a-5c34-a7a5-6614e8291acf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{7}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300564} {:answer/id #uuid "c3a1b26d-8ffa-5c97-9580-8122a3e8e4fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "63" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300940} {:answer/id #uuid "d92efac6-01ae-5b2e-8354-9a9f798d247d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301039}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4a359ac0-f626-5fc9-b0c1-01e620a91911" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300341} :answer-field/id #uuid "62bd16e6-f4e0-5d3c-b02e-ace76daef0c0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301543}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "468d9e20-789c-5294-9d35-e95d404e4f0e" :question/math-academy-id "q-32617" :question/problem "Given that \n$B=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 9 \\\\ 7 & 20\\end{bmatrix}$ \nhas the LU decomposition $B=LU$, then ${l}_{21}\\cdot {u}_{12}=$" :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $B$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $B$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 9 \\\\ 7 & 20\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{7}{3}){R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{7}{3} & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot B \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 9 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}B=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}B & =U \\\\ B & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ B & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{7}{3} & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ B & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{7}{3} & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{L}{{\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 9 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{21}=\\frac{7}{3},{u}_{12}=9,$ and ${l}_{21}\\cdot {u}_{12}=21.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345029 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119853 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2c1a562-1b3d-5180-9fef-e20ea916b17a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying LU Factorizations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3aff888b-3abd-519a-b020-976dc34f4245" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468100} {:answer/id #uuid "25a7ba29-bc65-581b-9964-6c11c222d79f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468101}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3aff888b-3abd-519a-b020-976dc34f4245" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468100} :answer-field/id #uuid "4f47bd49-5b77-5103-8d2d-487667211ef5" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468771} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7cbf7e8b-34e0-5ba3-bf96-0aa8c395b97b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468102} {:answer/id #uuid "a74eb240-f723-5f61-8cdf-f327609c7cf2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468103}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7cbf7e8b-34e0-5ba3-bf96-0aa8c395b97b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468102} :answer-field/id #uuid "1002dbbd-d4c2-58a4-9d7a-a603394b05ab" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468772} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "323a7811-4035-5802-b65f-fa37ee80f272" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468104} {:answer/id #uuid "6196d8dc-3375-5b48-89b1-f58fc5a8344c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468105}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6196d8dc-3375-5b48-89b1-f58fc5a8344c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468105} :answer-field/id #uuid "3c3ed68e-ffbd-5c3c-9b32-90b080f28a48" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468773}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "89a636cd-a216-5d73-bd3f-7f9cbe4badf4" :question/math-academy-id "q-75116" :question/problem "Which of the following products is the LU factorization of a matrix $C?$\n- **I.** $C=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n- **II.** $C=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n- **III.** $C=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Remember that in the LU factorization of a matrix $C,$ we write $C=LU$ where the matrix $L$ is a unit lower triangular matrix (a lower triangular matrix that has only $1$'s on the main diagonal) and the matrix $U$ is an upper triangular matrix:\n\n$$\nC=\\overset{L}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ ∗ & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot \\overset{U}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}∗ & ∗ \\\\ 0 & ∗\\end{bmatrix}}}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each product in turn.\n\n- The first product is *not* in $LU$ form  because  the matrix on the left is not a unit lower triangular matrix, as its main diagonal contains a number not equal to $1.$\n\n$$\nC=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\times \n$$\n\n- The second product is *not* in $LU$ form. Although we have a product of an upper triangular matrix on the left and a unit lower triangular matrix on the right, the order of multiplication is not correct (instead, we need a unit lower triangular matrix on the left and an upper triangular matrix on the right).\n\n$$\nC=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\times \n$$\n\n- The third product is in $LU$ form because the left matrix is a unit lower triangular matrix, and the right matrix is an upper triangular matrix. \n\n$$\nC=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\,✓\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345030 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119853 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2c1a562-1b3d-5180-9fef-e20ea916b17a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying LU Factorizations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9e49e9